Letting $x=-4$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-4+2)^n}{(n+1)\cdot(-2)^n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-2)^n}{(n+1)\cdot(-2)^n} \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{1}{n+1}
\end{align}$

By the integral test, we know this series diverges.

Letting $x=0$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(0+2)^n}{(n+1)\cdot(-2)^n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{2^n}{(n+1)\cdot(-2)^n} \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }  \dfrac{(-1)^n \cdot2^n}{(n+1)\cdot2^n} \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-1)^n}{n+1}
\end{align}$

By the alternating series test, we know this series converges.
